        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR> 
There is evidence that sleep is often impaired in microgravity.  Some crewmembers state that arousals are common and sleep quality is poor.  As a result, crewmembers use sedatives for sleep and do not wake refreshed, which may result in diminished work efficiency during the day.    
<P> 
Respiration and sleep are strongly coupled.  Changes in the neurological control of breathing are known to affect sleep patterns and result in periodic breathing, upper airway obstruction, apneic periods and arterial hypoxemia.  Possible changes in the chemoreceptive control of ventilation, caused by exposure to microgravity, may also contribute to alterations in the sleep pattern.  Recent studies on Spacelab Life Sciences-1 (SLS-1) showed that microgravity greatly alters pulmonary function.  Marked changes in blood flow, ventilation, gas exchange and lung volumes were observed.  On Spacelab D-2 and LMS, these studies were extended and showed that the relative contributions of the rib cage and abdomen to breathing were also altered in microgravity, and that modest exercise has no adverse effects on lung function in microgravity. <P> During Neurolab, two sleep experiments were integrated into a series of sleep studies and awake studies to evaluate the normal sleep patterns of crewmembers before, during and after space flight and to identify the factors that contribute to sleep disturbances in microgravity.  The first experiment was "Clinical Trial of Melatonin as a Hypnotic for the Neurolab Crew," and was designed to study the use of melatonin as a hypnotic agent for the treatment of sleep disturbances that occur during space flight.  "Sleep and Respiration in Microgravity," the second experiment, was designed to study the various aspects of respiratory function during sleep and wakefulness. <P> The objectives of the Sleep and Respiration in Microgravity experiment were to assess the pattern of chest and abdominal wall movement during normal ventilation and hyperventilation, and to assess how ventilatory response is affected by disrupted sleep. 
<P> 
APPROACH:<br>  
Sleep Studies<BR> Respiration was measured during sleep by instrumenting the subject with the Respiratory Inductance Plethysmograph (RIP) Suit and pulse oximeter, allowing continuous measurement of both the rib cage and abdominal motion, as well as arterial oxygen saturation.  In addition, subjects were fitted with an electrocardiograph (ECG), an electroencephalograph (EEG), an electrooculograph (EOG), a nasal airflow temperature sensor and an ingestible body temperature sensor.  From these sensors, measurements were taken of changes in ventilation, relative rib cage and abdominal contribution to ventilation, thoraco-abdominal asynchrony, sympathetic and parasympathetic contributions to heart rate variability, and the coupling between respiration and heart rate, all as a function of sleep stage.  Measurements were taken with and without the administration of melatonin preflight and inflight. <P> Awake Studies<BR> Pulmonary Function Test (PFT):<BR> The preflight and postflight Pulmonary Function Test (PFT) sequences deviated from the inflight protocol in that the subject performed the sequence in the standing and supine positions.  The subject also underwent a hypoxic ventilatory depression (HVD) test with two levels of hypoxia (10% inspired oxygen and 12% inspired oxygen).  The HVD test was not part of the PFT sequence; it was a stand alone test with a separate set of hardware and data systems.  The HVD test required the subject to rebreathe a low oxygen gas mixture from a bag in which carbon dioxide was actively removed to maintain isocapnia in the face of increased ventilation and decreasing oxygen.
<P>
The subject was instrumented with ECG electrodes, RIP Suit and pulse oximeter.  RIP signals were continuously recorded for ventilation, ventilatory timing, and relative rib cage and abdominal contributions to breathing throughout the PFT sequence.  The hardware system centers around a bag-in-box system.   Gases are dispensed into the bags within the box, and the subject controls the breathing path based on prompts presented on a microcomputer display.  Flow was measured with a Fleisch #2 pneumotachograph in the wall of the bag-in-box which was coupled to a Validyne MP-45 differential pressure transducer.  Gas concentrations were measured at the lips of the subject with a quadrupole mass spectrometer (GASMAP). 
<P> 
The subject performed a rebreathing (REB) maneuver from a gas mixture containing 10% argon, 0.6% acetylene, 0.3% carbon monoxide, 21% oxygen and balance nitrogen.  From the insoluble argon, lung volume was determined; from the soluble acetylene, the cardiac output and pulmonary tissue volume were determined; and from the highly absorbed carbon monoxide, the pulmonary diffusing capacity was determined.   
<P>
 A resting gas exchange (RGE) maneuver was then performed.  The subject breathed air for 5 minutes during which time gas exchange and ventilatory timing were determined.  ECG and blood pressure were recorded, allowing determination of the coupling between heart rate, respiration and blood pressure.  At the end of the test, a vital capacity slow exhalation was performed and the range of ventilation-perfusion ratios in the lung was determined.  Several determinations of the inspiratory occlusion pressure at 100 milliseconds (P100) were also made during the quiet breathing phase.  <P> P100 was measured during resting tidal breathing, and during the hypercapnic and hypoxic response tests.  At the beginning of an inspiration, the breathing path was briefly occluded to measure the inspiration airway pressure.  The P100 was determined from this pressure. <P> To determine control of ventilation (hypercapnic ventilatory response), the subject breathed room air for one minute then switched to the rebreathing bag containing 5% carbon dioxide, 60% oxygen and balance nitrogen.  The subject rebreathed for approximately 4 minutes, or until the carbon dioxide level reached 10%, or until the subject voluntarily terminated the test.  During this period, carbon dioxide and oxygen were monitored, establishing the carbon dioxide stimulus to breathe on a breath-by-breath basis.  
<P>
 To determine hypoxic ventilatory response (HVR), the rebreathing bag was filled with a volume of test gas (1 to 2% carbon dioxide, 23% oxygen, balance nitrogen) equal to the subject's vital capacity plus 1 liter.  The subject breathed normally for 3 minutes, providing baseline resting gas exchange data to determine average end-tidal carbon dioxide values.  The subject then took three deep breaths to ensure adequate mixing with the gas in the rebreathing bag, and then breathed normally.  As isocapnia is maintained, the oxygen concentration in the rebreathing gas mixture decreases proportionally to the subject's oxygen consumption.  Termination of the HVR test occurred when either the end-tidal oxygen reached 40 torr, arterial oxygen saturation decreased to 65-70% (this is subject dependent), or when the subject evidenced any distress.  Several determinations of the P100 were also made during the test, providing a measure of respiratory drive. 
<P> 
RESULTS: <br> 
Microgravity exposure greatly reduces the number of sleep related apneas and hypopneas, significantly reduces snoring, and reduces the number of respiratory arousals in normal, healthy individuals. These changes were probably due to the elimination of the gravitationally induced changes in the upper airway anatomical structures. Indeed, the reduction in the total number of arousals in microgravity resulted almost entirely from the large reduction in respiratory-related arousals. From these data investigators can infer that gravity plays a dominant role in the increase in upper airway resistance and obstruction that occurs after the transition to the supine posture and during all stages of sleep.
<P>
It has long been recognized that gravity might play a role in some forms of sleep-disordered breathing (especially obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)). However, these data provide the first direct evidence that the effect of gravity on the upper airways is dominant in causing obstruction. As such, the results may prove useful increasing the emphasis on postural treatments for OSA. The simple expedient of decreasing the rearward gravitational effect on the upper airways by encouraging sleep in a semi-upright posture may prove more acceptable to some patients than other treatments such as continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP).  Microgravity also resulted in a significant reduction in the hypoxic ventilatory response; however, the change that occurred was no different to that seen in the supine position. In contrast to the hypoxic response there was no change in the response to inhaled CO2 caused by either the supine position or by microgravity.
<P>
Thus the ventilatory control mechanisms in microgravity in these healthy subjects were much the same as they were in the supine position on the ground. The similarity is borne out by the similar numbers of central apneas and hypopneas observed in microgravity and on the ground, since central events result primarily from the ventilatory control system.  In conclusion, our data suggest that any sleep disruption caused by spaceflight or by microgravity has origins unrelated to the respiratory system.<P>
        </div>

